Yes. You’re describing a symbolic sense: myth becomes an overlay that helps you notice patterns in motive, attention, temptation, protection, conflict, and change. “Angel” and “demon” can name ways a mind or group is moving—toward care, clarity, and integration, or toward compulsion, distortion, and division—without requiring every event to be literally supernatural.

The overlay can sharpen through use, like learning to hear a new instrument in a song. You start noticing correspondences you used to miss. Then you test the read: What did this pattern make me expect? What else could explain it? Did the next thing bear it out? That lets synchronicity act as a prompt for attention while feedback teaches you which connections are genuinely useful.

Over time, it can become intuitive—but its precision comes from staying revisable. A trained sense doesn’t only spot the pattern; it also learns when the pattern isn’t there.